== The Fifth Sermon

#quote[And all tears will be wiped away from all faces.—Isa 25:8.]

Not only will death be swallowed up in victory, but God “will wipe away all tears from all eyes.” Religion will be religion; good things will be good things. Nothing will pass under false names. All tears will be wiped away. We have many causes of tears now. In the world there is a continual raising of clouds that distill into drops of tears. Even if we had nothing outside us to raise a vapor to be distilled in tears, we are able to raise up mists from our own mists, from our own doubts and conflicts within.

As we should weep for our own sins, so we should weep for the sins of others. We may see this in Jeremiah, where the prophet says, “Oh, that my head were a fountain of tears, that I might weep continually for the sins of my people” (Jer 9:1). And indeed good men are quick to weep, as the heathen man observes. They are quick to lament, not only for their own sins, but for the sins and misery of another.

As for our blessed Savior himself, we never read that he laughed. We have heard that he wept, and for his very enemies: “O Jerusalem, Jerusalem” (Matt 23:37). He shed tears for those who shed his blood. Tears were chief evidences of Christ’s sweetness of disposition, as was his becoming man, and a curse, and dying for us, and his making so much of little children, and calling to him all who were weary and heavy laden, and never refusing anyone who came to him. That he wept especially for the miseries and afflictions of others showed his gracious and sweet disposition. And in heaven he is so full of sympathy in glory that when Paul persecuted the church, he said, “Why do you persecute Me?” (Acts 9:4). So though he is free from passion in heaven, he is not free from compassion, from sympathy with his church. And so every child of God is ready to grieve not only for his own sins and the misery that follows them, but for the sins and miseries of others. “My eyes shed streams of tears,” says the prophet David (Ps 119:136), when he saw that men broke the law of God, whom he loved.

A true natural child takes the disgrace of his father to heart. If we are not grieved to see our father disgraced, we are illegitimate children, not sons. Those who make a sport of sin, what are they? Alas! they do not have one spark of the spirit of adoption. They are not children, who rejoice at what they should grieve at.

So St Paul says, “I have often told you#super[+], and now say again even with tears: Many live as enemies of the cross of Christ” (Phil 3:18). When he saw some men preach against the cross of Christ, and others live as its enemies, whose end is damnation, he told them of it weeping.

We have cause, therefore, to mourn for the sins of others and for the miseries of others, whether we consider God, or the church, or ourselves. First, the love of God moves us to weep when we see him dishonored.

Second, if we love the church, we should mourn for any sins that may harm their salvation.

Does it not move any man to pity to see an ox go to the slaughter? to see a man otherwise gifted going to the slaughter by sinning against his conscience? to see a habitual swearer, an unclean person, a profane wretch, covering himself with pride as with a garment, scorning God and the world and all? Can a Christian look on this, see flesh and blood like himself, under the gospel, in a cursed condition that cannot be escaped without serious repentance, and not be affected by it? Can a man see a poor donkey fall under a burden and not help to lift it up, and yet see a man falling to hell and not be affected by it? So we see we have cause enough for tears. And as there is cause, so we should feel it. We ought to take the afflictions of Joseph to heart. He is a dead man who has no feeling of this kind. If we go to the body and state, or anything about a man, there is cause for grief. Does not every member have many diseases? And are not our lives a kind of hospital, some sick of one thing, some of another? But as there is cause, we should feel it. We are flesh and not stones. It is therefore a foolish opinion to be wooden and brutish, as if to outface sorrow and grief were a glory.

Use 1. When our Savior was sent into the world, Christi dolor, dolor maximus [the grief of Christ was the greatest grief]; there would be no patience without feeling. Away, then, with that iron, that flinty philosophy that thinks it a virtue to be stupid, and, as the apostle says, “without natural affection” (Rom 1:31). He counts it the greatest judgment of God on the soul, yet they would have it a virtue. Why should I strike them any more? says God; they have no sense, no feeling (Isa 1:5).

The proud philosopher thought it was not philosophical to weep, a proud Stoic disposition; but Christians desire it.

And therefore we ought to labor to be more feeling, so that we may make our peace and reverence the justice of God, and be more aware of him afterward. It is most true that Sapiens miser, plus miser: the wiser any man is, the more he feels misery. And therefore, of all men, the best men have the most grief, because they have the quickest senses. They are not made stupid with insensibility and hardness, to bear it bravely, as the world does; but they take in the cause of grief with grief. And as they have a more sanctified judgment than other men, so they have a wiser affection of love and a livelier life of grace. Where there is life, there is feeling; and where there is a clear sight or cause of grief, there is most grief. Therefore the best men have the most grief, because they are the most judicious, the most loving.

Then, of all others, they have the most grace to bear it out. Therefore, considering there is cause of grief continually in ourselves and in others, we ought to labor to feel it; otherwise it would be no favor to have tears wiped away.

So there is cause for tears, and tears are a duty of Christians who feel the cause, both of sin and of misery, upon one and another.

Use 2. And as it is an unavoidable grief, so it is good that we should grieve. We must stoop to God’s course, we must bring our hearts to it, and pray (since our necessities and sins call for this dispensation, that we must be under correction, that he will make us feel his rod) that he would make good his covenant of grace, “to take away our stony hearts, and give us hearts of flesh” (Ezek 11:19), so that we may feel.

Most graces are founded on affection, and all graces are only affections sanctified. What would become of grace if we had no affections? Therefore, as there is cause of grief, and of tears from grief, we ought to grieve. It is a condition and a duty: a condition following misery, and a duty following our condition.

Beware of what hinders a feeling of troubles and judgments, that is, hardness of heart, forgetfulness, and studying to put away sorrow with sin. For we ought to feel, and ought to labor to feel, so as to know the meaning of every cross in ourselves and others.

But suppose we have crosses, and we must feel them; then it follows that “God will wipe away all tears from our eyes.” Is there nothing for the present, no ground of comfort? Yes. As we ought to feel grief, so we ought to feel what there is to rejoice in for the present, especially if we consider the time to come. The life of a Christian is a strange kind of life. He ought to grieve, and he ought to rejoice. He has occasion for both, and he ought to receive both; for what we ought to aim at especially is joy, and if we grieve, it is so that afterward we may rejoice. We must feel any affliction so that we may rejoice afterward, and we ought to labor for it. For is not the joy of the Lord our strength? Are we not fittest to do service when our spirits are most enlarged? And is it not a credit to religion when we walk in the comfort of the Holy Spirit? Is it not a scandal when we droop under the cross? We ought to feel, yet not so as to forget matter of joy and comfort. And therefore, as we ought to grieve, so we ought, when we have grieved, to keep up the soul with the consideration of joy for the present as much as we can; indeed, to pick out matter of comfort from the very cross. That is the heart of a Christian, not only to rejoice in other matters, but to pick comfort out of grief. God allows me to fall into this or that condition: it is a fruit of his fatherly love. He might allow me to run the broad way, to be given up to a reprobate mind and a hard heart, but he does not do so. Pick out matter of comfort from grief.

Then consider the presence of God in it. Indeed, I have matter of grief, but I find God moderating it. It might be far worse; it is his mercy that I am not consumed. I find God doing me good by it; I find myself better for it; I cannot well be without it. Who would not labor to feel a cross when he looks up to God’s cross, and justice, and mercy? He has more cause to rejoice than to grieve in the very cross itself.

But especially mark what the Holy Spirit says here. We ought not to be cast down too much by any cross, considering that God “will wipe away all tears from our eyes,” that is, all natural tears, and the miseries of this life. There will be no more misery, no more sickness, no more trouble.

And then all tears that arise from the consideration of sin, and of misery following sin. Death is the accomplishment of all mortification. It is a comfort that we will not always lead this conflicting life, but the war between the flesh and the spirit will be ended; the sense of it will be removed. One day we will be out of Satan’s reach and the world’s reach, which is a great comfort to consider. Whatever the cause is, the cause will be removed before long. If the cause is desertion, because God leaves us comfortless, we will be with the Lord forever hereafter. If the cause is separation from friends, why, we will all meet together before long, and be in heaven forever. If the cause is our own sins, we will cease hereafter to offend God, and Christ will be all in all. Now sin is almost all in all; sin and corruption hold great sway in us. If the matter of our grief is the sins of others and the afflictions of others, there is no sin in heaven: “nothing unclean will enter there” (Rev 21:27). The souls of perfect men are there, and all are of one mind. There is no opposition to goodness; there all will go one way. There, however little they can agree here, all will have mutual solace and contentment in one another, they in us and we in them, and that forever. You#super[+] cannot name or imagine a cause of tears but it will be removed there. Indeed, the more tears we have shed here, the more comfort we will have. As our troubles are increased here, our consolation will increase. What we suffer here, if it is for a good cause, will work for us “an eternal weight of glory that is far beyond comparison” (2 Cor 4:17). We say April showers bring forth May flowers. It is a common saying, from the experience of common life. It is true in religion. The more tears we shed in the April of our lives, the more sweet comfort we will have hereafter. If no tears are shed here, no flowers are to be gathered there. And therefore, besides deliverance from trouble, here is comfort: God will take away all cause of grief, and all kinds of grief whatever.

And therefore think of it in this way.

The next thing to be considered is the order. First we must shed tears, and then they must be wiped away. After a storm, a calm; after sowing in tears comes reaping in joy. What is the reason for that order? Reason 1. The reason is our own necessity. Since the fall we are in such a frame and condition that we cannot be put into a good frame of grace without much pain. The truths of God must cross us, and afflictions must join with them. For the sins contracted by pleasure must be dissolved by pain. Repentance must cost us tears. We may thank ourselves if we have brought ourselves into a sinful course. As for the necessity of this order, a diseased person must not be cured till he feels some smart of his wounds.

Reason 2. Again, consider that it is for our increase of comfort afterward that God will have us shed tears and then have our tears wiped away, because we feel joy and comfort more after sorrow. We cannot feel the joys of heaven unless we feel the contrary here. And therefore, of all men, heaven will be most heaven to those who have had their portion of crosses and afflictions here. First, therefore, tears are shed, and then they must be wiped away, because joy is then most felt. As it is with the wickedest of all men, they are most miserable who have been happiest, because their soul is enlarged by their happiness to take in sorrow more quickly and keenly; so those who have been most miserable here will have most joy hereafter.

Use 1. Now for use. Here is not only the mercy of God in Christ, but his tender mercy: though our life is full of tears, which we have brought on ourselves, yet God stoops so low as to wipe our eyes, like a father or mother. His mercy is a sweet and tender mercy. And, as the psalmist says, when we are sick “he makes our beds in our sickness” (Ps 41:3). Christ will come and serve those who watch and serve him; indeed, he will wait on them and “sup with them” (Rev 3:20). He is not only mercy and goodness, but there is in him tender mercy, the deepest compassion. He not only gives matter of joy and comfort, but he will act like a tender-hearted mother, wiping away all tears from our eyes. We cannot grasp the tenderness of God’s love, the pity and mercy of God toward those who are his and are afflicted in the world, especially in a good cause. However many their tears are, if they are penitent tears, he will wipe them all away.

And since we must shed tears here so that we may be comforted hereafter, let us take care that in this life we do not judge by sight, but by faith. “If we live by sight, we are of all men most wretched” (1 Cor 15:19). In the world the children of God are most miserable, and of the children of God, the best saints. Who has more cause for tears than the best saints? It is only seedtime here. While seedtime continues, there are tears. The farmer, while it is seedtime, cannot do his work without trouble. The minister cannot do his work but he is forced to take the sins of the times to heart, and to see his work go backward. Governors of families and such carry their seed weeping. Indeed, the best men at times cannot do good without trouble in themselves and a conflict with corruptions. No good is sown here but it is sown in tears; yet take no offense at this: “God will wipe away all tears.”

The Head of the church, our blessed Savior, and all his gracious apostles, what a life they lived! The glorious martyrs who sealed the truth with their blood! And therefore, as the apostle says, “If our happiness is here only, we are of all men most miserable” (1 Cor 15:19). If we judge by sight, we will condemn the generation of the righteous. We live by sight when, seeing anyone cast down with the sight of sin, the sense of temptation, or distress of conscience, we think him forlorn. Oh, beware of that! For those who shed tears here, God will wipe them all away. “Woe to those who laugh now, for they will mourn hereafter” (Luke 6:25). Though we weep here, yet matter of joy enough will spring up hereafter. “Afflictions will yield a quiet fruit of righteousness to those who are trained by them” (Heb 12:11). We may not see their fruits at once, but afterward. And therefore let us not be discouraged by anything we can suffer here, or for the church, if we see her under pressure. As darkness is sown for the wicked, and the foundation of their eternal torment is laid in their joy, so the ground and foundation of all a godly man’s joy is laid in tears. “Blessed are those who mourn, for they will be comforted” (Matt 5:4). Yet for the present there is more matter of joy than of grief, if we look with both eyes. For we ought to have double eyes, one to feel our grief, as we must, the other to see our comfort, so that we are not overwhelmed by grief. There is a sorrow to death, an excessive sorrow. It is unthankfulness to God to forget our comforts, as it is stupidity to forget our sorrow. Take us at the worst: do we not have more cause for joy than for sorrow? Mark Rom 5:1: “Since we have been justified through faith, we have peace with God,” and “we rejoice in the hope of glory.” Indeed, afterward he says, “we also rejoice in our sufferings.” And why? On what ground? “Knowing that tribulations bring proven character, and proven character hope, and hope does not put us to shame.” Now we rejoice in God reconciled in Christ. So as we ought to look with one eye on the grief, so that we may have ground to exercise grace, which we are not capable of without feeling, so we must look to the grounds of joy. Our life is woven of matter of sorrow and joy; and as it is woven of both, our affections should feel both, so that they may better take in the grounds of comfort.

When the day of persecution approaches, this will make us comforted, for our life is a valley of tears; and shall we not go through this valley of tears to this mountain where all tears will be wiped away from all eyes? When we are dejected by the loss of any friend, they say, as Christ said to the women, “Do not weep for me” (Luke 23:28). They are happy, “and all tears are wiped away from their eyes.” And therefore, as it is matter of comfort while we live, so it is ground of comfort when we die. For there is occasion of sorrow in death, in parting with friends and the comforts of this world. Then tears are shed in more abundance, and then we think back on former sins, and there is a renewing of repentance more than at other times; yet then we are near the time of joy, and nearest the fulfillment of the promise that “all tears will be wiped away.”

And so you#super[+] have the whole state of a Christian life: an afflicted condition. Yes, but it is a comforting condition. The more afflictions here, the more comfort here, but especially hereafter. The life of a carnal man is all in misery. If he falls to joy, he is all joy; if to sorrow, he is all sorrow. He has nothing to hold him up. He is like Nabal: he sinks like a piece of lead to the bottom of the sea (1 Sam 25:37, 38); like Ahithophel, down he goes (2 Sam 17:23). When he is in a merry mood, he is nothing but joy. But a Christian’s state and disposition are both mixed. He has ground of sorrow for his own sins, and for the sins and miseries of the times. So he has matter of comfort for the present, in the favor of God, in the pardon of sins, in the presence of God, in God’s delivering him from trouble. He has special ground of joy in the hope of glory in the time to come. Therefore, as we have a mixed state, let us labor for a mixed disposition, and labor to be in a joyful frame: so to grieve as to raise matter of joy out of it. And when we would rejoice, let us grieve first, for joy is sown in grief. The best method of joy is to take away all that disturbs our joy. Search the bottom of the heart! See what sin is unconfessed, unrepented of! Spread it before God; ask God to pardon it and to seal the pardon! When our souls are searched to the bottom, then out of that sorrow springs joy; and out of these sighs and groans that cannot be expressed comes joy unspeakable and full of glory. If a man would be joyful, let him labor to weep first, so that what interrupts his joy may be taken away. Those who would be joyful, and will not search to the bottom, must be brought back to sorrow with shame. When we would rejoice in earnest, let us judge ourselves, so that we may not be judged by the Lord. Let us mourn for our sins, and then lay hold on the promise that “all who mourn for sin will be comforted” (Matt 5:4). And blessed are those who shed tears here, for all tears will be wiped away.

We are apt to wrong ourselves, both good and bad: for the good think, if they are in misery, that they will always be so; the bad, if they are in prosperity, that they will always be so, and they bless themselves in it. Now the joy of the hypocrites is like the “crackling of thorns” (Eccl 7:6), and the grief of the godly is only short. And therefore let not the wicked fool themselves with groundless hopes, nor the godly vex themselves with needless fears; but put away the fancy that troubles will continue long. Time is only short, and before long God “will wipe away all tears from our eyes.” No mists, no clouds, will reach to heaven. The state in heaven will be like the state of the heavens, where there is no cloud, but all is pure, all serene. Therefore in Christianity consider not the beginning of things but their ends. “Mark the end of the upright, for the end of the upright is peace” (Ps 37:37). Ways take their praise from the point at which they end. “If by any means I may attain to the resurrection of the dead,” says Paul (Phil 3:11). Through thick and thin, fair and foul, rough winds, a dry or a bloody death: if by any means I may come to the resurrection of the dead, the first degree of glory, all is well. It is a good way that ends well. Non qua, sed quo: consider not by what way he brings us to heaven, but where he brings us. If he brings us to heaven through a valley of tears, it does not matter; for in heaven “all tears will be wiped from our eyes.” And therefore Christianity is called wisdom. “And wisdom is justified by her children” (Matt 11:19). What is the chief point of wisdom? To look home to the end, and to direct all means to that end. He is wise who is wise for eternity. The wicked will have their payment here. “But woe to those who laugh, for they will mourn,” says Christ (Luke 6:25). They will not wait for ground of joy hereafter, but will have present payment. But though the ways of Christians are foul and wet with tears, yet blessed are they; for God “will wipe away all tears from their eyes.” “Comfort one another with these words” (1 Thess 4:18).
